Date: Fri, 2 Apr 93 01:32:53 -0800
From: mcpherso@lumina.ucsd.edu (John McPherson)
Subject: I**2:  Measuring "intelligence" (was VR: question ...)

Note:  My subject header "I**2" means "Intelligence Increase".

   Recently, there have been researchers/thinkers who reject a
one-dimensional measure of intelligence in favor of multi-
dimensional models.  I may not have their names right, but I
believe they are:  Dr. J. P. Guilford ("Way Beyond the IQ") and
Dr. Howard Gardner ("Frames of Mind").  Presumably, their systems
would yield an intelligence "profile", ie. a collection of measures
of "cognitive" abilities in rather different modes of operation.
I believe that Gardner came up with 7 distinct areas of human
intelligence (eg., math/logic, musical, spatial/visual, etc.).

   Years ago there was a researcher, whose name I believe was/is
Dr. John Ertl, who was attempting to measure general "intelligence"
via evoked cortical responses.  I have a reference at home if anyone
is interested.  If his system is accurate, then a person's "true" 
intelligence could be measured very quickly, and perhaps some sort
of bio-feedback-loop could be set up to reward ECRs associated with
"higher intelligence".  If there's anything to the theories of 
multiple intelligences, then perhaps ECR measures could be associated
with each of the different modes of operation.


   Before this thread progresses much further, if it does, then
it might be useful to define what we mean by "intelligence".  I'm
still not clear on what it means.  When in doubt, use the dictionary!

"Intelligence:  the ability to learn and understand, or to deal with
   new or challenging situations;  mental acuteness  (Britannica-
   Webster, 1981).   the capacity to acquire and apply knowledge
   (American Heritage, 1980)."

   I love this definition because it is wide open.  It applies to
learning new skills and tasks, which could involve a person's whole
being in some activity.  It applies to how well, and how quickly,
one can size up a situation and figure out what to do.  It applies
to conversation, and to efficiency in gathering information and
putting it together.

   As I wrote the above, I got excited.  I would love to become
more intelligent, or at least to be more consistently intelligent
at a high level in my day-to-day living.  To what extent can one
train oneself to be more intelligent?  Can intelligence itself be
"learned"?  How far can one boost one's peak intelligence, and by
what means?  Measuring intelligence lets a person know where they
are in terms of "mental" capacities.  If accurate measures can
be made, then a person may be able to keep track of their progress
in a program designed to increase their intelligence.

   Taking a clue from a previous poster, several more specific
questions come to mind:

How might one increase their short-term memory capacity?
How might one fine-tune their ability to perceive spatial relationships?
In what ways can one develop clearer perception of linguistic structures?
How might one develop a better ability to compute "mentally"?
How might one become more creative?
What is involved, generally, in solving problems, and how might one
   become more proficient at doing so?


