Subject: Irish Ethnicity

Tim Starr writes:

> (preface: Did Dale Worley really preach to us how the Irish potato famine
> was all because of the monoculture?  Here I thought everybody knew that
> it was the Brits that compelled the monoculture!)

Right.  As in most catastrophes, there's plenty of blame to go around.  At
the risk of sounding earnest (yuck), I'd say that the Irish famine has a
number of useful lessons for the list.  For one thing, it's the first
unambiguous, non-war-related (not directly, anyway: excursus on request)
example of famine as a product of state politics rather than actual
dearth; in anything remotely resembling a free market, the famine would
have been greatly ameliorated, if not avoided.  

We also see the pitfalls of a powerful technology badly implemented.  The
advent of potato culture certainly bettered the lot of peasants all over
Europe by providing a high-yield, low-maintainance, nutritious staple -
almost a nanofood in the context of the peasant diet of the time.  In
Ireland, however, the economic distortions imposed by conquest and absentee
landlordism led to monoculture, and worse, a monoculture resting on a
very slender gene bank.  The real tragedy was that they got away with it
long enough to bulge the population, so that when the Blight hit, it found
more victims. 

Faith, an' I'm after sayin' it's a great ammo for anarcho-capitalists, an'
soberin' cautionary for th'technophiles, it is... 

