Date: Fri, 2 Apr 93 08:21:41 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: I**2:  Measuring "intelligence"

>   Recently, there have been researchers/thinkers who reject a
>one-dimensional measure of intelligence in favor of multi-
>dimensional models.  
>John McPherson   

I'd like to share a story about multi-intelligence testing (I'm not
commenting on such testing per se).

I once heard a public teaching professional advocating the use of
multi-dimensional intelligence tests on a public radio talk show (which,
you'll note, I had helped to pay for with my tax dollars  8-).  She said
these tests were preferrable to uni-dimensional IQ tests because they were
more "inclusive"; that is, they celebrated the uniquely different skills of
each child.  No one, she implied, would be "dumb."  Students would only be
"differently intelligent."

I was astounded (though in retrospect I should have expected this sort of
reasoning out of a public teaching professional).  Using a
multi-dimensional intelligence test does not, of course, mean that every
person will score high in at least one of the dimensions.  Some people will
be found to be brilliant in all respects.  Others will be found
multi-dimensionally stupid.  All else being equal, I would expect a
multi-dimensional test to flatten the curve of normal distribution, but not
eliminate it.  

I concluded that the public teaching professional in question was
"differently clued" with regard to intelligence testing.  I suspect that
her thinking was clouded by "compassion addiction."

