Date: Fri, 2 Apr 93 11:03:59 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Dangers of Emphasizing IQ and IQ Testing

Maybe I'm just feeling a bit grumpy today after all my satires of
yesterday, but I have another negative comment to make.

Concentration on IQs, past IQ achievements, and techniques for increasing
IQ (usually via superficial self-help books that emphasize such nonsense as
speed reading and rote memorization of trivia) is an "entropic" trend, in
my opinion.

Whatever ideas one may have (or not have) are not furthered by announcing
one's IQ.

The pathetic state of Mensa, which has more UFO abductees, witches, and
overall neurotics than any other group I know, is an example of where this
focus can take you. Frustrated low-level clerks announcing widely their
supposed IQs!


Atl, Church of All Worlds, and IQ

In my reading for the Great Pagan Trial, I read with great interest [Adler,
"Drawing Down the Moon," p. 290] the history of the Rand-Heinlein-inspired
"Atl" group. Formed in the early 60s, it was named after the apparent
significance of the 3-letter combination "Atl" in such significant words
as: "Atlantis," "Atlas Shrugged," and "Atlantic." [Duh]

After grokking each other in water ceremonies ("Thou art God"), they
quickly realized that what made them all so special was that they were
*smarter* than the people around them! The Atlans thus became obsessed with
IQ and IQ testing.

They changed directions a couple of times, had some schisms, and eventually
became the "Church of All Worlds." (Some of you may remember that the ExI
received a large donation from "an anonymous CAW member." Apparently they
grokked our intelligence.)

[I believe the saga of the Atl-CAW movement has some lessons for the
Extropians movement, but I'll save most of my comments on this for another
essay.]

But what does a focus on intelligence and IQ scores give a group? Not much,
as we can't alter our own innate intelligence, nor do bullshit books on
improving IQ scores have much to do with real achievement.

I don't really know or care what IQ score I got whenever I was last tested,
perhaps in the 3rd or 4th grade. And I stopped telling people my SAT scores
around 1972 or so, when I realized just how tacky and pointless it was.

I briefly dated a very talkative woman--who was undeniably bright in a kind
of surface-level, fast-talking way--who told me at least *three* times that
her IQ was 163. What a turn-off! 

- Tim


