The best review of all this is:

    Bennett, C. (1990) "The Thermodynamics of Computation - a Review",
    in Maxwell's Demon - Entropy, Information, Computing, ed. H. Leff, 
    A. Rex, Princeton Univ. Press. pp. 213-248.

Did I tell you folks get confused about this?  Now even Tim May has
stumbled.  He writes:

>(It is not clear to me, nor do others seem to have achieved final
>conclusions, if any large computations that reach "new"
>conclusions--presumably the reason to do computations in the first
>place--can be done reversibly on a global scale. However, it may certainly
>be the case that parts of a computation are reversible and parts are
>not...some have speculated that the brain achieves a high computational
>throughput on a "better than kT" basis, perhaps by using locally reversible
>computations.)  ...
>
>Whether large-scale computations, such as might be found in one of
>Drexler's general assembler "breadboxes," can be made to use mostly
>reversible logic is an open question.

These are not open questions, and we "experts" have reached final
conclusions here.  *Any* irreversible computer program, in any known
language, can be run on a reversible computer, with a polynomial
performance penalty of arbitrarily low order, and with total entropy
losses at worst linear in the speed at which the hardware is run. See:

    Bennett, C. (1989) "Time/Space Trade-Offs For Reversible
    Computation", SIAM J. Comput, V18, N4, August, pp. 766-776.

Furthermore, simple modifications to Drexler's nanocomputer design
allow it to be such a reversible computer (though Drexler does not
discuss this in his book).  

What was an open question, at least in my mind, was what additional
entropy costs must be paid for a computer interacting with the world
around it, rather than just crunching in it's input.  This was the
question I tried to answer in my "Reversible Agents" paper (published
in the proceedings Tim recommended).

Robin Hanson

