The clumsiness of the "prefix" system is revealed by the current thread
over the talents or lack of talents by Marilyn Vos Savant:

"VR: question re: P. Science"

Now I have no idea what the original thread was all about, whether in fact
it dealt with "VR" or not, and don't have any idea what "question re: P.
Science" means. Was this an article in "Popular Science"? Perhaps "pseudo
science"? What?

This title is almost worthless.

Better titles might be:

"Marilyn Vos Savant--Most Intelligent Person?"

"Multidimensional IQ Tests"

"IQ: Marilyn Vos Savant"

...and so on, with or without prefixes.

I suggest people carefully consider what the most descriptive title of
their current posting may be and use *that*. If the thread changes its
title, so be it. If people want some "stickiness" or "inertia," so that
threads don't change name with every posting, they can use the previous
title for as long as it remains mostly applicable.

I still say clear titles are more important than prefixes, even for sorting
purposes (for example, all posts labelled "Marilyn Vos Savant...." would
fall together in alphabetical order and could be simultaneously deleted
unread). But I suspect we can all agree that flaky prefixes are the worst
of all. 

